Original commit body from 97a128fcb4a84c8a66172f22b911a7290bc6afe2:

Two tests declared a mode, got PAST the gate, and then let auto-pr do real
work -- push, PR lookup, the lot. Locally that failed fast and they passed
in about a second. In CI it hung and
test_foreground_declared_gets_past_the_gate died on

    subprocess.TimeoutExpired: ... timed out after 120 seconds

The defect is mine and it is in the fixture, not the gate: the tests
depended on the network failing QUICKLY, which is not a property any
environment owes them. A test whose pass depends on how fast an external
call fails is a flake with a good day.

Both now run from `dev`. The gate sits at auto-pr:1955 and the
protected-branch refusal at :2001, so a declared run gets past the gate and
then stops immediately on a check that touches nothing outside the repo.
The assertion also tightened from "not failed_mode_undeclared" to "IS
failed_protected_branch" -- the weaker form was satisfied by a run that
hung, crashed or never started, which is precisely how the first version
managed to be green locally while being meaningless.

The pty control got the same treatment. It had the same exposure and only
escaped by luck of timing in the one CI run that caught its sibling.

